  "text": "**Solution 3 (α = −1/4) is most likely correct, though I could not verify it against the literature.**\n\n- **Solution 1 (−1/2)** is unlikely. Cut points are never inside a loop, so they survive every erasure order. That gives a lower bound of about n(log n)^{-1/2} on the LLF length, which is compatible with −1/2. But Solution 1 then asserts that LLF saturates this bound, and its argument for the conditional survival probability, 1 − O(1/k) per scale, is asserted rather than derived.\n- **Solution 2 (−1/3)** is unlikely. It just transfers the chronological exponent to LLF with no mechanism. The chronological −1/3 is Lawler's result; a different erasure order should generically change the exponent.\n- **Solution 3 (−1/4)** has a concrete mechanism. The two arms of a candidate cut point are each thinned by the larger loops already erased. Survival then becomes a two-sided non-intersection of thinned paths. Its calibration reproduces the known chronological exponent −1/3 and the cut-point exponent −1/2. It also respects the cut-point lower bound. Its heuristic is not a proof, but it is the most coherent of the three, and I recall the LLF result in d = 4 being stated with exponent −1/4.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
